Subject: mtd: rawnand: meson: write-protect the vendor boot region

Kept as its own patch so that it is visible in review and cannot be
dropped by accident along with a rebase.

The first thing this driver will meet on a real Meson8b board is a chip
the Amlogic bootloader wrote and still depends on, on a board whose only
recovery path is the USB Burning Tool -- which restores firmware but not
the per-device key blobs that live in that region. So there are two
gates, and they are deliberately not the same gate.

meson_nand.allow_write (default 0) clears MTD_WRITEABLE on the master
for any SoC marked experimental. Partitions copy the master's flags, so
mtdchar writes, MEMERASE and UBI all get -EROFS through every path, and
the module parameter is the single place a user says "I accept this".

The address guard is not overridable. write_protect_end is derived at
attach time from the geometry the chip reports, using the vendor
driver's own units -- BOOT_COPY_NUM (4) x BOOT_PAGES_PER_COPY (256)
pages of u-boot copies plus RESERVED_BLOCK_CNT (48) blocks of BBT, key
and config data, all from drivers/amlnf/include/phynand.h, with slack
because that reserved window is counted in *good* blocks and therefore
slides upward with the factory bad blocks. Deriving it rather than
hardcoding a byte offset is what keeps it correct on a part nobody has
identified yet -- but a derived window also shrinks by exactly the
factor by which nand_scan() under-reports the geometry, and
mis-detection is the most likely failure mode on an unidentified die
driven by an unvalidated port. So the result is clamped to a 16MiB
floor, which is comfortably past 4 x 256 boot pages on any plausible
geometry.

Getting the guard onto every path that can modify the chip takes five
hooks, not one, because mtd->_erase is not the only way in:

  meson_nfc_exec_op()        refuses a NAND_CMD_ERASE1 or NAND_CMD_SEQIN
                             whose row address falls inside the region.
                             This is the innermost gate and the only one
                             nand_base cannot walk around:
                             nand_block_markbad_lowlevel() calls
                             nand_erase_nand() DIRECTLY rather than
                             through mtd->_erase, so with only an
                             ->_erase wrapper a MEMSETBADBLOCK ioctl on
                             block 0 erases the bootloader -- which is
                             exactly what "nandtest --markbad" issues
                             after an erase is refused. Both opcodes
                             carry the row the same way and both reach
                             the chip from exactly one place:
                             nand_erase_op() emits ERASE1 as a CMD plus
                             a row-address-only ADDR, and
                             nand_exec_prog_page_op() emits SEQIN with
                             the two column cycles of
                             nand_fill_column_cycles() ahead of the row.
                             So the target block is recoverable here
                             exactly, by skipping two cycles for SEQIN
                             and none for ERASE1.

  mtd->_block_markbad        refuses before nand_block_markbad_lowlevel()
                             is entered at all, so the caller gets
                             -EROFS instead of a silently ineffective
                             mark.

  ecc.write_oob{,_raw}       nand_do_write_oob() dispatches straight to
                             these, so neither the page-program guard
                             below nor anything on mtd->_write_oob sees
                             an OOB-only program. Both the MEMWRITEOOB
                             ioctl and nand_default_block_markbad() get
                             there, and the bytes they would rewrite
                             inside a boot page are covered by the ECC
                             the ROM checks.

  meson_nfc_write_page_sub() refuses hwecc and raw page programs.

  mtd->_erase                refuses erases arriving the normal way, so
                             the error comes back from the ioctl rather
                             than from an op two layers down.

Upstream's secure-regions mechanism was considered and rejected. A
`secure-regions = <0x0 0x...>` property on the nand@0 node is enforced
inside nand_erase_nand(), nand_do_write_oob(), nand_do_write_ops() and
nand_isbad_bbm(), so it would cover the erase path that motivated all of
this, in fewer lines and without touching the driver. But it also gates
the READ paths (nand_do_read_ops() and nand_do_read_oob() both return
-EIO for a secured region), and reading that region is the entire point
of this port: dumping the vendor pages and comparing them against a dump
taken with the vendor bootloader is the only way to find out whether the
ECC and scrambler settings are right. It would also break probe outright
-- create_bbt() propagates the -EIO out of scan_block_fast(), so
nand_scan() fails before an mtd device is ever registered. And being a
DT property it protects only boards that remember to write it, whereas
this is a property of the SoC family. Read-only-plus-address-guard keeps
the region readable and protects every meson8/8b board whether or not
its author knew to ask.

Both the MTD_WRITEABLE clear and the mtd op wrappers have to be
installed after nand_scan() returns: nand_scan_tail() runs after
->attach_chip() and assigns mtd->_erase, mtd->_block_markbad and, via
nanddev_init(), mtd->flags outright.

What this does NOT protect against: an on-flash bad block table.
nand_bbt.c calls nand_erase_nand() directly too, from write_bbt(). That
path is only reachable with nand-on-flash-bbt, and it places the table
in blocks at the end of the chip rather than at the start, so it does
not overlap this region -- but it is not gated here either.

diff --git a/drivers/mtd/nand/raw/meson_nand.c b/drivers/mtd/nand/raw/meson_nand.c
--- a/drivers/mtd/nand/raw/meson_nand.c
+++ b/drivers/mtd/nand/raw/meson_nand.c
@@ -145,6 +145,35 @@
 #define NFC_COLUMN_ADDR_1	0
 
 /*
+ * Extent of the region the Amlogic bootloader owns, in the vendor driver's own
+ * units: BOOT_COPY_NUM (4) x BOOT_PAGES_PER_COPY (256) pages of u-boot copies,
+ * then RESERVED_BLOCK_CNT (48) blocks of BBT, key and config data, all from
+ * drivers/amlnf/include/phynand.h.  The reserved window is counted in *good*
+ * blocks, so it slides further up the chip the more factory bad blocks fall
+ * inside it -- hence the slack.  Expressed in pages and blocks rather than
+ * bytes because the fitted part is not identified on the board this was
+ * written for, so it has to be derived from the geometry the chip reports.
+ */
+#define MESON8_VENDOR_BOOT_PAGES		1024
+#define MESON8_VENDOR_RESERVED_BLOCKS	64
+
+/*
+ * ...but only as a lower bound on the region, never as the whole answer.  A
+ * geometry-derived window shrinks by exactly the factor by which nand_scan()
+ * under-reports the page or erase size, and mis-detection is the single most
+ * likely failure mode on an unidentified die driven by an unvalidated port.
+ * 16MiB is what this platform already reserves ahead of the first partition on
+ * its eMMC variant, and it is comfortably past the 4 x 256 boot pages on any
+ * plausible geometry.
+ */
+#define MESON8_VENDOR_MIN_BYTES		(16 * 1024 * 1024)
+
+static bool meson_nfc_allow_write;
+module_param_named(allow_write, meson_nfc_allow_write, bool, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(allow_write,
+		 "Permit erase and program on SoCs whose NAND port is unvalidated (default: the MTD is read-only)");
+
+/*
  * Off by default on every SoC that sets .soft_waitrdy.  No Amlogic code has
  * ever enabled the NFC ready/busy interrupt on the M8 family, and no meson8b
  * device tree carries an interrupt for this controller, so the polling path is
@@ -172,6 +201,11 @@
 	u8 *data_buf;
 	__le64 *info_buf;
 	u32 info_buf_size;
+	loff_t write_protect_end;
+	int (*orig_erase)(struct mtd_info *mtd, struct erase_info *instr);
+	int (*orig_write_oob)(struct nand_chip *chip, int page);
+	int (*orig_write_oob_raw)(struct nand_chip *chip, int page);
+	int (*orig_block_markbad)(struct mtd_info *mtd, loff_t ofs);
 	u32 nsels;
 	u8 sels[] __counted_by(nsels);
 };
@@ -321,6 +355,20 @@
 	return container_of(nand, struct meson_nfc_nand_chip, nand);
 }
 
+/*
+ * True when @page lies inside the region the boot ROM and the vendor
+ * bootloader own.  Zero write_protect_end means the guard is not armed at all,
+ * which is the case on every SoC not marked .experimental.
+ */
+static bool meson_nfc_page_is_protected(struct nand_chip *nand, int page)
+{
+	struct meson_nfc_nand_chip *meson_chip = to_meson_nand(nand);
+	struct mtd_info *mtd = nand_to_mtd(nand);
+
+	return meson_chip->write_protect_end &&
+	       (loff_t)page * mtd->writesize < meson_chip->write_protect_end;
+}
+
 static void meson_nfc_select_chip(struct nand_chip *nand, int chip)
 {
 	struct meson_nfc_nand_chip *meson_chip = to_meson_nand(nand);
@@ -870,6 +918,13 @@
 	u32 cmd;
 	int ret;
 
+	if (meson_nfc_page_is_protected(nand, page)) {
+		dev_err(nfc->dev,
+			"refusing to program page %d: the vendor bootloader region ends at 0x%llx\n",
+			page, meson_chip->write_protect_end);
+		return -EROFS;
+	}
+
 	meson_nfc_select_chip(nand, nand->cur_cs);
 
 	data_len =  mtd->writesize + mtd->oobsize;
@@ -1181,6 +1236,61 @@
 	if (check_only)
 		return 0;
 
+	/*
+	 * The innermost gate.  mtd->_erase is not the single entry to an erase:
+	 * nand_block_markbad_lowlevel() calls nand_erase_nand() directly, so a
+	 * MEMSETBADBLOCK ioctl on block 0 would erase the bootloader with every
+	 * mtd-level wrapper still in place.  Both destructive primitives reach
+	 * the chip as an op carrying deassert_wp, and both encode the target row
+	 * the same way, so decoding it here catches whatever nand_base grows a
+	 * new caller for: nand_erase_op() emits ERASE1 with a row-only address,
+	 * and nand_exec_prog_page_op() emits SEQIN with the two column cycles of
+	 * nand_fill_column_cycles() ahead of the row.
+	 */
+	if (meson_chip->write_protect_end && op->deassert_wp) {
+		for (op_id = 0; op_id + 1 < op->ninstrs; op_id++) {
+			const struct nand_op_instr *addr = &op->instrs[op_id + 1];
+			unsigned int page = 0;
+			int skip;
+
+			instr = &op->instrs[op_id];
+			if (instr->type != NAND_OP_CMD_INSTR ||
+			    addr->type != NAND_OP_ADDR_INSTR)
+				continue;
+
+			switch (instr->ctx.cmd.opcode) {
+			case NAND_CMD_ERASE1:
+				skip = 0;
+				break;
+			case NAND_CMD_SEQIN:
+				/*
+				 * nand_fill_column_cycles() emits one column
+				 * cycle on a small-page chip and two otherwise,
+				 * so the row does not start at a fixed index.
+				 */
+				skip = nand_to_mtd(nand)->writesize <= 512 ? 1 : 2;
+				break;
+			default:
+				continue;
+			}
+
+			/* A row address is two or three cycles wide. */
+			if (addr->ctx.addr.naddrs < skip + 2 ||
+			    addr->ctx.addr.naddrs > skip + 3)
+				continue;
+
+			for (i = addr->ctx.addr.naddrs - 1; i >= skip; i--)
+				page = (page << 8) | addr->ctx.addr.addrs[i];
+
+			if (meson_nfc_page_is_protected(nand, page)) {
+				dev_err(nfc->dev,
+					"refusing to modify the block holding page %u: the vendor bootloader region ends at 0x%llx\n",
+					page, meson_chip->write_protect_end);
+				return -EROFS;
+			}
+		}
+	}
+
 	meson_nfc_select_chip(nand, op->cs);
 	for (op_id = 0; op_id < op->ninstrs; op_id++) {
 		instr = &op->instrs[op_id];
@@ -1542,6 +1652,36 @@
 	meson_nfc_free_buffer(nand);
 }
 
+static int meson_nfc_write_oob_guard(struct nand_chip *nand, int page)
+{
+	struct meson_nfc_nand_chip *meson_chip = to_meson_nand(nand);
+	struct meson_nfc *nfc = nand_get_controller_data(nand);
+
+	if (meson_nfc_page_is_protected(nand, page)) {
+		dev_err(nfc->dev,
+			"refusing to write the OOB of page %d: the vendor bootloader region ends at 0x%llx\n",
+			page, meson_chip->write_protect_end);
+		return -EROFS;
+	}
+
+	return meson_chip->orig_write_oob(nand, page);
+}
+
+static int meson_nfc_write_oob_raw_guard(struct nand_chip *nand, int page)
+{
+	struct meson_nfc_nand_chip *meson_chip = to_meson_nand(nand);
+	struct meson_nfc *nfc = nand_get_controller_data(nand);
+
+	if (meson_nfc_page_is_protected(nand, page)) {
+		dev_err(nfc->dev,
+			"refusing to write the OOB of page %d: the vendor bootloader region ends at 0x%llx\n",
+			page, meson_chip->write_protect_end);
+		return -EROFS;
+	}
+
+	return meson_chip->orig_write_oob_raw(nand, page);
+}
+
 static int meson_nand_attach_chip(struct nand_chip *nand)
 {
 	struct meson_nfc *nfc = nand_get_controller_data(nand);
@@ -1590,6 +1730,17 @@
 		return -EOPNOTSUPP;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Arm the address guard as early as possible: everything installed
+	 * below keys off write_protect_end being non-zero.
+	 */
+	if (nfc->data->experimental)
+		meson_chip->write_protect_end =
+			max_t(loff_t,
+			      (loff_t)MESON8_VENDOR_BOOT_PAGES * mtd->writesize +
+			      (loff_t)MESON8_VENDOR_RESERVED_BLOCKS * mtd->erasesize,
+			      MESON8_VENDOR_MIN_BYTES);
+
 	if (nand->bbt_options & NAND_BBT_USE_FLASH)
 		nand->bbt_options |= NAND_BBT_NO_OOB;
 
@@ -1624,6 +1775,20 @@
 	nand->ecc.write_oob_raw = nand_write_oob_std;
 	nand->ecc.write_oob = nand_write_oob_std;
 
+	/*
+	 * nand_do_write_oob() dispatches straight to ecc.write_oob{,_raw}, so
+	 * neither meson_nfc_write_page_sub() nor anything installed on
+	 * mtd->_write_oob sees an OOB-only program.  Both the MEMWRITEOOB ioctl
+	 * and nand_default_block_markbad() get here, and the bytes they would
+	 * rewrite inside a boot page are covered by the ECC the ROM checks.
+	 */
+	if (meson_chip->write_protect_end) {
+		meson_chip->orig_write_oob = nand->ecc.write_oob;
+		meson_chip->orig_write_oob_raw = nand->ecc.write_oob_raw;
+		nand->ecc.write_oob = meson_nfc_write_oob_guard;
+		nand->ecc.write_oob_raw = meson_nfc_write_oob_raw_guard;
+	}
+
 	nand->ecc.read_page_raw = meson_nfc_read_page_raw;
 	nand->ecc.read_page = meson_nfc_read_page_hwecc;
 	nand->ecc.read_oob_raw = meson_nfc_read_oob_raw;
@@ -1652,6 +1817,42 @@
 	return ret;
 }
 
+static int meson_nfc_erase_guard(struct mtd_info *mtd, struct erase_info *instr)
+{
+	struct meson_nfc_nand_chip *meson_chip = to_meson_nand(mtd_to_nand(mtd));
+
+	if (instr->addr < meson_chip->write_protect_end) {
+		dev_err(&mtd->dev,
+			"refusing to erase 0x%llx: the vendor bootloader region ends at 0x%llx\n",
+			instr->addr, meson_chip->write_protect_end);
+		return -EROFS;
+	}
+
+	return meson_chip->orig_erase(mtd, instr);
+}
+
+/*
+ * mtd_block_markbad() is a write path in disguise, and the most dangerous one
+ * here: nand_block_markbad_lowlevel() ERASES the block first, and it calls
+ * nand_erase_nand() directly rather than through mtd->_erase, so the erase
+ * wrapper above never sees it.  "nandtest --markbad" issues exactly this after
+ * an erase is refused, which without this wrapper turns a refused erase of
+ * block 0 into a completed one.
+ */
+static int meson_nfc_block_markbad_guard(struct mtd_info *mtd, loff_t ofs)
+{
+	struct meson_nfc_nand_chip *meson_chip = to_meson_nand(mtd_to_nand(mtd));
+
+	if (ofs < meson_chip->write_protect_end) {
+		dev_err(&mtd->dev,
+			"refusing to mark 0x%llx bad: that would erase it, and the vendor bootloader region ends at 0x%llx\n",
+			ofs, meson_chip->write_protect_end);
+		return -EROFS;
+	}
+
+	return meson_chip->orig_block_markbad(mtd, ofs);
+}
+
 static const struct nand_controller_ops meson_nand_controller_ops = {
 	.attach_chip = meson_nand_attach_chip,
 	.detach_chip = meson_nand_detach_chip,
@@ -1721,6 +1922,31 @@
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
+	/*
+	 * These have to happen here rather than in ->attach_chip():
+	 * nand_scan_tail() runs after attach and assigns mtd->_erase,
+	 * mtd->_block_markbad and, via nanddev_init(), mtd->flags outright, so
+	 * anything set earlier is simply overwritten.
+	 */
+	if (meson_chip->write_protect_end) {
+		meson_chip->orig_erase = mtd->_erase;
+		mtd->_erase = meson_nfc_erase_guard;
+		meson_chip->orig_block_markbad = mtd->_block_markbad;
+		mtd->_block_markbad = meson_nfc_block_markbad_guard;
+
+		if (!meson_nfc_allow_write) {
+			/* partitions inherit this, so it covers them too */
+			mtd->flags &= ~MTD_WRITEABLE;
+			dev_warn(dev,
+				 "%s: read-only, this SoC's NAND port has never been validated on real silicon; boot with meson_nand.allow_write=1 to override\n",
+				 mtd->name);
+		}
+
+		dev_info(dev,
+			 "%s: erase, program, OOB write and mark-bad are refused below 0x%llx whatever meson_nand.allow_write says\n",
+			 mtd->name, meson_chip->write_protect_end);
+	}
+
 	if (nand->options & NAND_IS_BOOT_MEDIUM) {
